# Caddy — PROD-PARITY edge. Serves the vite-BUILT frontend bundle statically over
# HTTPS/HTTP-2 — the exact topology a real deployment uses. Unlike the dev edge
# (docker/Caddyfile) it does NOT proxy the app shell to the vite dev server; it
# serves the built bundle from /srv/app. Layered via docker-compose.prod.yml so we
# can develop/test against production's shape and stop bugs hiding in the dev/prod
# gap (operator directive, 2026-07). See docs/prod-parity.md.
#
# TLS is the same locally-trusted setup as docker/Caddyfile.https: a mkcert cert
# pair at /certs/localhost.pem + /certs/localhost-key.pem (one-time host step, see
# docs/https-local.md — no domain or purchased cert needed locally). The /datasets
# and /api handles are SHARED with the dev edge via docker/Caddyfile.edge-snippets
# (imported below, T2-76), so the ONLY intentional prod/dev differences are:
# (1) built static bundle instead of the vite server, and (2) HTTP/2 instead of
# HTTP/1.1 — H2 multiplexes the pyramid's many parallel {z}/{x}/{y} byte-range
# reads over one connection instead of queueing them behind the browser's
# ~6-connection HTTP/1.1 cap.
{
	# Opt-in TLS edge; an explicit cert is supplied via `tls` below. admin off
	# matches the other edges.
	admin off
}

# Load the shared edge snippet definitions (datasets_edge, api_edge) so the site
# block below can `import` them by name (T2-76). Mounted alongside this Caddyfile.
import Caddyfile.edge-snippets

# The base compose file maps host :8080 -> container :80 and an override cannot
# UN-map an inherited port. Without an explicit http:// site, Caddy's automatic
# HTTP->HTTPS redirect points at https://localhost (no port — its https_port is
# the in-container 443), which is unreachable from the host; anyone hitting the
# familiar dev URL http://localhost:8080 would get a dead redirect. Send them to
# the real external HTTPS edge instead.
http://localhost {
	redir https://localhost:8443{uri}
}

https://localhost {
	# Locally-trusted cert pair issued by mkcert on the HOST (one-time
	# `mkcert -install` + `mkcert localhost 127.0.0.1 ::1`), placed at these stable
	# filenames under ./docker/certs. See docs/https-local.md.
	tls /certs/localhost.pem /certs/localhost-key.pem

	# Static versioned dataset assets (D-30) and the /api reverse-proxy are shared,
	# byte-identical across all three edge profiles — imported from the shared
	# snippet file so an edge change lands once (T2-76). See docker/Caddyfile.edge-snippets.
	import datasets_edge
	import api_edge

	# App shell: the vite-built SPA bundle from disk (NOT the dev server). try_files
	# falls back to index.html so client-side routes resolve. No explicit cache
	# header here: index.html must stay revalidatable so a fresh build is picked up
	# (vite's own asset filenames are content-hashed, so the bundle is safe to cache
	# by the browser's default heuristics; aggressive asset caching can be layered
	# later once verified live). `encode` is scoped to THIS block (the text bundle:
	# the ~850 kB JS compresses to ~230 kB) and deliberately NOT applied to
	# /datasets — those are already-compressed WebP/PMTiles served via HTTP Range,
	# which must not be re-encoded.
	handle {
		root * /srv/app
		encode gzip zstd
		try_files {path} /index.html
		file_server
	}
}
